
# Docker Learning Notes
## From Beginner → Docker Compose + PostgreSQL

---

# 1. What is Docker?

Docker is a platform used to package and run applications in isolated environments called containers.

Without Docker:

    Application
        ↓
    Requires Java
    Requires PostgreSQL
    Requires specific libraries
    Requires specific configuration
        ↓
    "It works on my machine"

With Docker:

    Application
        +
    Dependencies
        +
    Configuration
        ↓
    Docker Image
        ↓
    Docker Container

The goal is to make applications easier to build, run, move, and deploy consistently.

---

# 2. Docker Important Terms

The most important Docker concepts:

1. Image
2. Container
3. Dockerfile
4. Docker Build
5. Docker Registry
6. Container Lifecycle
7. Ports
8. Volumes
9. Bind Mounts
10. Networks
11. Docker Compose
12. Environment Variables
13. .env
14. Persistent Data

---

# 3. Docker Image

## Definition

A Docker Image is a packaged, read-only template used to create containers.

Example:

    nginx
    postgres:18
    ubuntu
    node
    openjdk

An image contains things needed to run an application:

    Application
    Dependencies
    Libraries
    Configuration
    Files
    Runtime

Example:

    nginx image
        ↓
    creates Nginx container

---

# 4. Docker Container

## Definition

A Container is a running instance of a Docker Image.

Think:

    Image
      ↓
    Container

Example:

    nginx image
       ↓
    my-nginx container

One image can create multiple containers.

Example:

    nginx image
       ├── container-1
       ├── container-2
       └── container-3

---

# 5. Image vs Container

| Image | Container |
|---|---|
| Template | Running instance |
| Read-only template | Has writable container layer |
| Used to create containers | Runs the application |
| Can be reused | Can be started/stopped/deleted |
| Example: nginx | Example: my-nginx |

Simple example:

    Image = Blueprint
    Container = Building created from blueprint

---

# 6. Docker Registry

A registry stores Docker images.

Example:

    Docker Hub

When we run:

    docker pull nginx

Docker downloads the Nginx image from a registry if it is not already available locally.

---

# 7. Check Docker Version

Command:

    docker --version

Example:

    Docker version 28.1.1

---

# 8. Docker Service

On Linux, Docker normally runs as a system service.

Check:

    systemctl status docker

Start:

    sudo systemctl start docker

Stop:

    sudo systemctl stop docker

Restart:

    sudo systemctl restart docker

Docker daemon:

    dockerd

The Docker CLI communicates with the Docker daemon.

Architecture:

    docker command
         ↓
    Docker CLI
         ↓
    Docker daemon
         ↓
    Containers / Images / Networks / Volumes

---

# 9. Docker Permission on Linux

Docker socket:

    /var/run/docker.sock

Usually:

    root docker

If your user is added to the docker group:

    sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

Refresh current shell:

    newgrp docker

Then:

    docker ps

can work without sudo.

---

# 10. Docker Images

List images:

    docker images

or:

    docker image ls

Example:

    REPOSITORY    TAG       IMAGE ID       SIZE
    nginx         latest    xxxxx          170MB

---

# 11. Pull an Image

Command:

    docker pull nginx

This downloads the image.

Usually:

    Docker CLI
       ↓
    Docker Hub
       ↓
    nginx image
       ↓
    Local machine

---

# 12. Run a Container

Command:

    docker run nginx

This generally:

    1. Finds image
    2. Downloads image if necessary
    3. Creates container
    4. Starts container

Important:

    docker run = create + start

---

# 13. Run in Background

Command:

    docker run -d nginx

`-d` means:

    detached mode

The container runs in the background.

---

# 14. Give Container a Name

Command:

    docker run -d --name my-nginx nginx

Now container name:

    my-nginx

Instead of a random Docker-generated name.

---

# 15. List Running Containers

Command:

    docker ps

Shows only running containers.

---

# 16. List All Containers

Command:

    docker ps -a

Shows:

    Running
    Stopped
    Exited
    Created

Example:

    docker ps -a

---

# 17. Container Lifecycle

Basic lifecycle:

    Image
      ↓
    docker create
      ↓
    Created
      ↓
    docker start
      ↓
    Running
      ↓
    docker stop
      ↓
    Stopped
      ↓
    docker start
      ↓
    Running

Delete:

    docker rm container-name

---

# 18. docker create

Creates a container but does NOT start it.

Example:

    docker create --name test-nginx nginx

Result:

    Container created
    Container not running

Start:

    docker start test-nginx

---

# 19. docker run

Creates and starts a new container.

Example:

    docker run -d --name my-nginx nginx

Equivalent conceptually to:

    docker create ...
    docker start ...

---

# 20. Stop a Container

Command:

    docker stop my-nginx

The container stops but still exists.

Check:

    docker ps -a

---

# 21. Start a Stopped Container

Command:

    docker start my-nginx

The existing container starts again.

---

# 22. Remove a Container

Command:

    docker rm my-nginx

The container is deleted.

Important:

    Removing container
          DOES NOT
    automatically remove image

Image remains.

---

# 23. Force Remove

Command:

    docker rm -f my-nginx

Useful when the container is running.

`-f` = force

---

# 24. Container Shell

Enter a running container:

    docker exec -it my-nginx bash

Meaning:

    exec = execute command
    -i   = interactive
    -t   = terminal
    bash = start Bash shell

Inside:

    root@container:/#

---

# 25. Exit Container

Inside container:

    exit

This exits the shell.

IMPORTANT:

    exit

usually does NOT mean delete container.

It only exits the shell session.

---

# 26. Files Inside Containers

Example:

    /usr/share/nginx/html

For Nginx, HTML files are stored there.

Inside container:

    pwd

Shows current directory.

List files:

    ls

Detailed:

    ls -la

---

# 27. Container Storage

A container has a writable layer.

Example:

    Container
       ↓
    create file
       ↓
    file exists

If the container is stopped:

    file remains

If the container is deleted:

    file disappears

Therefore:

    Container storage
          ↓
    tied to container

For important data:

    Use Volume
    or
    Bind Mount

---

# 28. Dockerfile

A Dockerfile is a text file containing instructions for building a Docker image.

Example:

    Dockerfile

Content:

    FROM nginx:latest

    COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

---

# 29. FROM

Example:

    FROM nginx:latest

Means:

    Start from nginx image.

Another example:

    FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jdk

Means:

    Start from Java 17 image.

---

# 30. COPY

Example:

    COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

Meaning:

    Copy file from build context
        ↓
    Into image

Diagram:

    Linux project
        │
        └── index.html
                ↓
             COPY
                ↓
    Docker Image
        │
        └── /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

---

# 31. Build an Image

Inside directory containing Dockerfile:

    docker build -t my-nginx-app:1.0 .

Meaning:

    docker build
        ↓
    build image

    -t
        ↓
    give image name/tag

    my-nginx-app:1.0
        ↓
    image name + tag

    .
        ↓
    current directory = build context

---

# 32. Image Tags

Example:

    my-nginx-app:1.0

Breakdown:

    my-nginx-app
         ↓
    image repository/name

    1.0
         ↓
    tag/version

Example:

    my-nginx-app:1.0
    my-nginx-app:1.1
    my-nginx-app:2.0

`latest` is also a commonly used tag:

    nginx:latest

---

# 33. Docker Build Context

Command:

    docker build -t my-app .

The final `.` means:

    current directory

Docker can use files from the build context.

Example:

    project/
    ├── Dockerfile
    └── index.html

Run:

    docker build -t my-app .

Docker can COPY:

    index.html

because it is inside the build context.

---

# 34. Docker Build Cache

Docker builds images in layers.

Example:

    FROM nginx:latest
        ↓
    COPY index.html ...
        ↓
    Final image

If nothing changed:

    CACHED

If index.html changes:

    FROM nginx
       ↓
    cached

    COPY index.html
       ↓
    rebuilt

This makes builds faster.

---

# 35. Image History

Command:

    docker image history my-nginx-app:1.0

Shows image layers.

Example:

    COPY index.html
    CMD
    EXPOSE
    ENTRYPOINT
    RUN
    Base image

---

# 36. Important Image/Container Concept

Suppose:

    Container A
       ↓
    uses image version 1

Then rebuild the same tag:

    my-nginx-app:1.0

A new image ID may be created.

Existing Container A does NOT automatically change to the new image.

You need to recreate the container.

Typical process:

    docker rm -f old-container

    docker run ... my-nginx-app:1.0

---

# 37. Ports

Docker containers have their own network namespace.

Example:

    Nginx listens inside container:

    port 80

If we want to access it from Linux host:

    -p 8081:80

Meaning:

    HOST               CONTAINER

    8081   ─────────→   80

Diagram:

    Browser
       ↓
    localhost:8081
       ↓
    Docker
       ↓
    container:80
       ↓
    Nginx

---

# 38. Port Mapping

Command:

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx \
      -p 8081:80 \
      nginx

Then browser:

    http://localhost:8081

---

# 39. Why 8081 Instead of 8080?

If another application already uses:

    localhost:8080

Docker cannot also bind:

    host:8080

Example error:

    address already in use

So:

    -p 8081:80

means:

    Host 8081 → Container 80

---

# 40. PORTS Column

Example:

    80/tcp

Means:

    Port 80 exists inside container.

It does NOT mean host port 80 is published.

Example:

    0.0.0.0:8081->80/tcp

Means:

    Host 8081 → Container 80

---

# 41. Container Port vs Host Port

Very important:

    Host
    localhost:8081
         ↓
    Container
    :80

But container-to-container communication:

    Container A
         ↓
    web-server:80
         ↓
    Container B

The host port is not needed.

---

# 42. Docker Volume

A Docker Volume is persistent storage managed by Docker.

Create:

    docker volume create nginx-data

List:

    docker volume ls

Inspect:

    docker volume inspect nginx-data

---

# 43. Volume Mount

Example:

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx \
      -v nginx-data:/usr/share/nginx/html \
      nginx

Meaning:

    Docker volume
    nginx-data
         ↓
    Container
    /usr/share/nginx/html

---

# 44. Why Volumes?

Without volume:

    Container
       ↓
    database data
       ↓
    container deleted
       ↓
    data may be lost

With volume:

    PostgreSQL container
          ↓
    postgres-data volume
          ↓
    container deleted
          ↓
    volume remains
          ↓
    new PostgreSQL container
          ↓
    same data

---

# 45. Volume Persistence Test

Example:

    Container 1
       ↓
    writes file
       ↓
    volume
       ↓
    Container 1 deleted
       ↓
    volume remains
       ↓
    Container 2
       ↓
    same file available

---

# 46. docker compose down vs down -v

Very important.

    docker compose down

Usually removes:

    Containers
    Compose network

But named volumes remain.

    docker compose down -v

Removes:

    Containers
    Network
    Compose-managed volumes

BE CAREFUL with:

    docker compose down -v

especially when working with databases.

---

# 47. Bind Mount

A Bind Mount connects a real directory from your Linux machine to a container.

Example:

    Linux folder:
    ./html

        ↓

    Container:
    /usr/share/nginx/html

Command:

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx-bind \
      -p 8081:80 \
      -v "$(pwd)/html:/usr/share/nginx/html" \
      nginx

---

# 48. Bind Mount Diagram

    Linux machine

    nginx-practice/
        └── html/
            └── index.html
                  │
                  │ bind mount
                  ▼
    Container
        /usr/share/nginx/html/
            └── index.html
                  │
                  ▼
                Nginx

If Linux file changes:

    Linux index.html
          ↓
    container sees change
          ↓
    browser sees change

---

# 49. Volume vs Bind Mount

| Docker Volume | Bind Mount |
|---|---|
| Managed by Docker | Managed by user |
| Storage location controlled by Docker | User chooses directory |
| Good for databases | Good for development files |
| Persistent | Persistent |
| Example: postgres-data | Example: ./html |

Simple:

    Volume:

    Docker
      ↓
    storage

    Bind Mount:

    Your Linux folder
      ↓
    container

---

# 50. Docker Network

Docker networks allow containers to communicate.

List networks:

    docker network ls

Default networks usually include:

    bridge
    host
    none

---

# 51. Create Network

Command:

    docker network create practice-network

Now:

    practice-network
        ↓
    containers can join

---

# 52. Connect Container to Network

Example:

    docker run -d \
      --name web-server \
      --network practice-network \
      nginx

Another:

    docker run -d \
      --name client \
      --network practice-network \
      nginx

Diagram:

    practice-network
          │
       ┌──┴───┐
       ↓      ↓
    client   web-server

---

# 53. Inspect Network

Command:

    docker network inspect practice-network

Shows information such as:

    subnet
    gateway
    connected containers
    container IP addresses

Example:

    web-server → 172.18.0.2
    client     → 172.18.0.3

Do NOT normally hard-code these IPs.

---

# 54. Container-to-Container Communication

If two containers are on the same user-defined network:

    client
       │
       │
       ▼
    web-server

Inside client:

    curl http://web-server

Docker resolves:

    web-server
        ↓
    container IP

This is Docker's internal DNS/service-name resolution.

---

# 55. Why Not localhost?

Inside `client`:

    localhost

means:

    client itself

It does NOT mean:

    web-server

Correct:

    http://web-server

Incorrect for another container:

    http://localhost

---

# 56. Container-to-Container vs Host-to-Container

Host to container:

    Browser
       ↓
    localhost:8081
       ↓
    container:80

Container to container:

    client
       ↓
    web-server:80

No host port is required for the second case.

---

# 57. Docker Compose

Docker Compose allows us to define multiple services in a YAML file.

Instead of:

    docker network create ...

    docker run ...

    docker run ...

we define:

    compose.yaml

and run:

    docker compose up -d

---

# 58. Docker Compose File

Basic example:

    services:
      web:
        image: nginx
        ports:
          - "8082:80"

---

# 59. Compose Service

Example:

    services:
      web:
        image: nginx

`web` is the service name.

The service name can also become a hostname for communication between services.

Example:

    http://web

---

# 60. Docker Compose Network

If Compose contains:

    services:
      web:
        image: nginx

      client:
        image: nginx

Compose automatically creates a default network.

Example:

    compose-practice_default

Both services join that network.

Diagram:

    compose-practice_default
            │
        ┌───┴────┐
        ↓        ↓
       web     client

---

# 61. Compose Service Communication

Inside client:

    curl http://web

Why?

Because:

    web

is the service name.

Docker Compose provides DNS resolution.

---

# 62. Docker Compose Commands

Start:

    docker compose up

Start in background:

    docker compose up -d

Stop/remove:

    docker compose down

Show services:

    docker compose ps

Show all including stopped:

    docker compose ps -a

Validate/render configuration:

    docker compose config

---

# 63. Compose Logs

Useful command:

    docker compose logs

Specific service:

    docker compose logs postgres

Follow logs:

    docker compose logs -f postgres

`-f` means follow.

---

# 64. Compose Exec

Execute command inside service:

    docker compose exec postgres psql ...

Example:

    docker compose exec client bash

This is similar to:

    docker exec -it container-name bash

But Compose uses the service.

---

# 65. PostgreSQL with Docker Compose

Example:

    services:
      postgres:
        image: postgres:18

        environment:
          POSTGRES_DB: moneylog_db
          POSTGRES_USER: moneylog_user
          POSTGRES_PASSWORD: moneylog123

        volumes:
          - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql

    volumes:
      postgres-data:

---

# 66. PostgreSQL Environment Variables

For the official PostgreSQL image:

    POSTGRES_DB

Database name.

    POSTGRES_USER

Database user.

    POSTGRES_PASSWORD

Database password.

Example:

    POSTGRES_DB=moneylog_db
    POSTGRES_USER=moneylog_user
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=moneylog123

---

# 67. PostgreSQL Port

PostgreSQL normally listens on:

    5432

If Compose contains:

    ports:
      - "5432:5432"

then:

    Linux host 5432
          ↓
    PostgreSQL container 5432

But if there is NO ports section:

    5432/tcp

PostgreSQL is still reachable by other containers on the same Docker network.

---

# 68. PostgreSQL Container-to-Container Connection

Suppose:

    postgres:
      image: postgres:18

Another service:

    client:
      image: postgres:18

Both are on:

    compose-practice_default

Then client can connect using:

    postgres:5432

Example:

    psql \
      -h postgres \
      -U moneylog_user \
      -d moneylog_db

Important:

    -h postgres

`postgres` is the Compose service name.

NOT:

    localhost

NOT:

    changing container IP

---

# 69. PostgreSQL Persistence

PostgreSQL should use persistent storage.

Example:

    volumes:
      postgres-data:

And:

    postgres:
      volumes:
        - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql

Architecture:

    PostgreSQL container
          │
          ▼
    postgres-data
          │
          ▼
    persistent database storage

Container can be removed without automatically removing the named volume.

---

# 70. depends_on

Example:

    client:
      image: postgres:18
      depends_on:
        - postgres

Means:

    Start postgres service before client service.

Important:

`depends_on` does not automatically mean the database is fully ready to accept connections.

It mainly controls startup ordering unless health checks are configured.

---

# 71. Container Main Process

A very important Docker concept:

A container stays alive while its main process is running.

Example:

    client:
      image: postgres:18

The PostgreSQL image normally tries to start PostgreSQL.

If the required setup isn't provided, it can exit.

We used:

    command: ["sleep", "infinity"]

This made the client container stay alive.

Diagram:

    Container
        │
        └── PID 1
             │
             └── sleep infinity
                    │
                    └── keeps running

When PID 1 exits:

    Process exits
         ↓
    Container stops

---

# 72. command in Compose

Example:

    command: ["sleep", "infinity"]

This overrides the image's default command.

Useful for practice/debugging/testing.

---

# 73. Environment Variables

Instead of hard-coding:

    POSTGRES_DB: moneylog_db

we can use:

    POSTGRES_DB: ${POSTGRES_DB}

Compose gets the value from environment configuration.

---

# 74. .env File

Create:

    .env

Example:

    POSTGRES_DB=moneylog_db
    POSTGRES_USER=moneylog_user
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=moneylog123

Compose can automatically read `.env` in the Compose project directory for variable substitution.

---

# 75. Using .env in Compose

`.env`:

    POSTGRES_DB=moneylog_db
    POSTGRES_USER=moneylog_user
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=moneylog123

Compose:

    services:
      postgres:
        image: postgres:18

        environment:
          POSTGRES_DB: ${POSTGRES_DB}
          POSTGRES_USER: ${POSTGRES_USER}
          POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}

Compose substitutes:

    ${POSTGRES_DB}
          ↓
    moneylog_db

---

# 76. docker compose config

Command:

    docker compose config

This renders the final Compose configuration.

It is useful for checking:

    YAML structure
    variable substitution
    networks
    volumes
    services

For example:

    ${POSTGRES_DB}

can become:

    moneylog_db

IMPORTANT:

If the configuration contains secrets, `docker compose config` can display them.

Do not share production output containing real passwords.

---

# 77. .gitignore

Secrets should generally not be committed to Git.

Create:

    .gitignore

Add:

    .env

Meaning:

    Git should not track .env

Important:

`.gitignore` does NOT hide `.env` from Docker.

It only tells Git not to track it.

---

# 78. Important Difference

`.env`

    Provides configuration values to Compose.

`.gitignore`

    Prevents Git from tracking files.

They have completely different jobs.

---

# 79. Compose Project Naming

Suppose directory/project:

    compose-practice

Compose may create:

    compose-practice_default

network

and:

    compose-practice_postgres-data

volume

Container:

    compose-practice-postgres-1

The naming is generated from the Compose project and service names.

---

# 80. Full Compose Architecture We Practiced

We built:

    compose-practice
          │
          ├── Network
          │     └── compose-practice_default
          │
          ├── PostgreSQL container
          │     └── postgres:5432
          │
          ├── Client container
          │     └── psql client
          │
          └── Volume
                └── compose-practice_postgres-data

---

# 81. Full Container Communication Diagram

    ┌───────────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │              Docker Compose                  │
    │                                               │
    │        compose-practice_default               │
    │                                               │
    │   ┌───────────────┐                           │
    │   │    client     │                           │
    │   │               │                           │
    │   │ psql          │                           │
    │   └───────┬───────┘                           │
    │           │                                   │
    │           │ postgres:5432                    │
    │           ▼                                   │
    │   ┌───────────────┐                           │
    │   │   postgres    │                           │
    │   │               │                           │
    │   │ PostgreSQL    │                           │
    │   │     :5432     │                           │
    │   └───────┬───────┘                           │
    │           │                                   │
    │           ▼                                   │
    │   ┌─────────────────┐                         │
    │   │ postgres-data   │                         │
    │   │ Docker Volume   │                         │
    │   └─────────────────┘                         │
    │                                               │
    └───────────────────────────────────────────────┘

---

# 82. Host vs Docker Network

Very important concept.

HOST:

    localhost:8081
         │
         ▼
    Nginx container :80

DOCKER NETWORK:

    Spring Boot container
         │
         │ postgres:5432
         ▼
    PostgreSQL container

Inside Docker:

    localhost

means the current container.

---

# 83. Common Docker Commands

## Images

    docker images
    docker image ls
    docker pull nginx
    docker build -t my-app:1.0 .
    docker image history my-app:1.0
    docker rmi image-name

## Containers

    docker ps
    docker ps -a
    docker run nginx
    docker run -d nginx
    docker create nginx
    docker start container
    docker stop container
    docker rm container
    docker rm -f container
    docker exec -it container bash

## Networks

    docker network ls
    docker network create practice-network
    docker network inspect practice-network
    docker network rm practice-network

## Volumes

    docker volume ls
    docker volume create nginx-data
    docker volume inspect nginx-data
    docker volume rm nginx-data

## Compose

    docker compose up
    docker compose up -d
    docker compose down
    docker compose down -v
    docker compose ps
    docker compose ps -a
    docker compose logs
    docker compose config
    docker compose exec SERVICE COMMAND

---

# 84. Docker Command Mental Model

Think:

    docker run
         ↓
    "Create and start something"

    docker ps
         ↓
    "What is running?"

    docker ps -a
         ↓
    "What exists, running or stopped?"

    docker stop
         ↓
    "Stop it"

    docker start
         ↓
    "Start existing container"

    docker rm
         ↓
    "Delete container"

    docker images
         ↓
    "What images do I have?"

    docker build
         ↓
    "Build my own image"

    docker volume
         ↓
    "Manage persistent storage"

    docker network
         ↓
    "Manage container communication"

    docker compose
         ↓
    "Manage multiple services together"

---

# 85. Docker vs Virtual Machine

Docker container:

    Shares host kernel
    Lightweight
    Starts quickly
    Isolated process environment

Virtual Machine:

    Includes guest operating system
    More resource-heavy
    Stronger full-OS isolation

Simplified:

    VM:

    Hardware
       ↓
    Host OS
       ↓
    Hypervisor
       ↓
    Guest OS
       ↓
    Application


    Docker:

    Hardware
       ↓
    Host OS
       ↓
    Docker
       ↓
    Container
       ↓
    Application

---

# 86. Docker Image vs Dockerfile

Dockerfile:

    Instructions

Image:

    Result of building those instructions

Example:

    Dockerfile
       ↓
    docker build
       ↓
    Image
       ↓
    docker run
       ↓
    Container

---

# 87. Volume vs Image vs Container Storage

## Image

Contains application/files needed to create containers.

    Image
       ↓
    reusable

## Container writable layer

    Container
       ↓
    temporary changes
       ↓
    deleted with container

## Volume

    Volume
       ↓
    persistent data
       ↓
    independent of container

---

# 88. Bind Mount vs Volume

Use Volume when:

    Database
    Persistent application data
    Docker-managed storage

Use Bind Mount when:

    Development source code
    Local configuration
    Files you want to edit directly on host

---

# 89. Example Real Application Architecture

Later, MoneyLog can look like:

    ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
    │              Docker Compose             │
    │                                          │
    │   ┌────────────────┐                     │
    │   │ Spring Boot    │                     │
    │   │ Backend        │                     │
    │   │ :8080          │                     │
    │   └───────┬────────┘                     │
    │           │                              │
    │           │ postgres:5432                │
    │           ▼                              │
    │   ┌────────────────┐                     │
    │   │ PostgreSQL     │                     │
    │   │ :5432          │                     │
    │   └───────┬────────┘                     │
    │           │                              │
    │           ▼                              │
    │   ┌────────────────┐                     │
    │   │ postgres-data  │                     │
    │   │ Volume         │                     │
    │   └────────────────┘                     │
    │                                          │
    └──────────────────────────────────────────┘

Browser:

    Browser
       ↓
    localhost:8080
       ↓
    Spring Boot container

Spring Boot:

    jdbc:postgresql://postgres:5432/moneylog_db

NOT:

    jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/moneylog_db

because PostgreSQL is inside another container.

---

# 90. Typical Docker Development Flow

For a Spring Boot application:

    Source Code
         ↓
    Dockerfile
         ↓
    docker build
         ↓
    Docker Image
         ↓
    docker compose
         ↓
    Spring Boot Container
         │
         │ Docker Network
         ▼
    PostgreSQL Container
         │
         ▼
    PostgreSQL Volume

---

# 91. Troubleshooting Checklist

## Port already in use

Error:

    address already in use

Check:

    sudo lsof -i :8080

or:

    ss -ltnp | grep :8080

Then stop the process or use another host port.

---

## Container not running

Check:

    docker ps -a

Check logs:

    docker logs container-name

Compose:

    docker compose logs service-name

---

## Container exits immediately

Remember:

    Container runs while main process runs.

Check:

    docker ps -a

Look at:

    Exited (1)

Then:

    docker logs container-name

---

## Cannot connect between containers

Check:

    docker network ls

Then:

    docker network inspect network-name

Make sure both containers are on the same network.

With Compose, check:

    docker compose ps

and use the service name.

---

## Database data disappeared

Check whether a volume was used:

    docker volume ls

Be careful with:

    docker compose down -v

because `-v` removes Compose-managed volumes.

---

# 92. Commands We Practiced

## Nginx

    docker run -d --name my-nginx -p 8081:80 nginx

## Custom image

Dockerfile:

    FROM nginx:latest

    COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

Build:

    docker build -t my-nginx-app:1.0 .

Run:

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx-custom \
      -p 8081:80 \
      my-nginx-app:1.0

## Volume

    docker volume create nginx-data

    docker volume inspect nginx-data

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx-volume \
      -p 8081:80 \
      -v nginx-data:/usr/share/nginx/html \
      nginx

## Bind Mount

    mkdir html

    nano html/index.html

    docker run -d \
      --name my-nginx-bind \
      -p 8081:80 \
      -v "$(pwd)/html:/usr/share/nginx/html" \
      nginx

## Network

    docker network create practice-network

    docker run -d \
      --name web-server \
      --network practice-network \
      nginx

    docker run -d \
      --name client \
      --network practice-network \
      nginx

Inside client:

    curl http://web-server

## Compose

    docker compose up -d

    docker compose ps

    docker compose down

    docker compose config

    docker compose exec client bash

---

# 93. Our Compose PostgreSQL Practice

compose.yaml:

    services:
      postgres:
        image: postgres:18
        environment:
          POSTGRES_DB: ${POSTGRES_DB}
          POSTGRES_USER: ${POSTGRES_USER}
          POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}
        volumes:
          - postgres-data:/var/lib/postgresql

      client:
        image: postgres:18
        depends_on:
          - postgres
        command: ["sleep", "infinity"]

    volumes:
      postgres-data:

.env:

    POSTGRES_DB=moneylog_db
    POSTGRES_USER=moneylog_user
    POSTGRES_PASSWORD=moneylog123

.gitignore:

    .env

Start:

    docker compose up -d

Check:

    docker compose ps

Connect from client:

    docker compose exec client \
      psql \
      -h postgres \
      -U moneylog_user \
      -d moneylog_db

Inside PostgreSQL:

    \l

List databases.

    \dt

List tables.

    \q

Quit PostgreSQL.

---

# 94. The Most Important Docker Concepts to Remember

If you forget everything else, remember this:

    Dockerfile
        ↓
    Image
        ↓
    Container


    Volume
        ↓
    Persistent data


    Bind Mount
        ↓
    Host folder ↔ Container folder


    Network
        ↓
    Container ↔ Container


    Port mapping
        ↓
    Host ↔ Container


    Compose
        ↓
    Multiple containers/services together


    .env
        ↓
    Configuration/secrets


    Service name
        ↓
    Container-to-container hostname

